# Justification of the files attributed to the resume patch as "extra"

## Premise correction (read this first)
The brief says the resume patch touches 10 files and that its "extra 3 files" vs the completed
patch are `enum-queries`, `record-copy`, `sealed-variants`. The on-disk
`string-family-fix-resume/PATCH.diff` (mtime 00:47) touches **13** test files — the 10 listed in
the brief plus `std-string`, `string-buffer`, `value-type`. Against the completed patch's
12 files, the set difference is exactly **one file**:

- resume-only: `tests/ts/sealed-variants.test.ts`
- completed-only: (none)
- `tests/ts/enum-queries.test.ts` and `tests/ts/record-copy.test.ts` are in **both** patches,
  with **byte-identical** hunks (see patch-comparison.tsv). They were likely "extra" in an
  EARLIER iteration of the resume patch (before it converged on the same 12-file core); the
  final on-disk patch no longer diverges on them.

Verdicts, per file the parent asked about:

## 1. `tests/ts/enum-queries.test.ts` — IN BOTH PATCHES, identical hunk
Change: line 54 `pub fn from_name(name: &str) -> Option<FloatWidth>` →
`pub fn from_name(name: &UStr) -> Option<FloatWidth>`.
Generator output: `reference/rust/gen/boring/float_width.rs:42`
`pub fn from_name(name: &UStr) -> Option<FloatWidth>` (verified in the scratch tree, byte-identical
to both sibling worktrees' trees and to the p09 chainA-fixed3 snapshot).
Evidence the line was failing before: completed agent's before-48 census name
`enum value queries generated trees > Rust emits ALL, name, from_name and a literal range`;
reproduced in my local before targeted run. **Warranted in both patches; no divergence.**

## 2. `tests/ts/record-copy.test.ts` — IN BOTH PATCHES, identical hunk
