## 3. `tests/ts/sealed-variants.test.ts` — RESUME-ONLY, WARRANTED
Change: line 245, inside test `sealed variant sample trees carry the ruled declaration and
printed forms` (the test reads `reference/rust/gen/boring/sealed_variant_ops.rs` at line 220):
  - old: `expect(rust).toContain('return "NoneDrawKind".to_string();');`
  - new: `expect(rust).toContain('return UString::from("NoneDrawKind").to_ustring();');`
Generator output: `sealed_variant_ops.rs:35-36` (p09 chainA-fixed3 snapshot, mtime 2026-09-29
18:10; byte-identical in both sibling worktrees, mtime 2026-09-28 23:08):
    pub fn to_string(&self) -> UString {
        return UString::from("NoneDrawKind").to_ustring();
    }
The new expectation matches the generator **byte-for-byte** (note the odd `.to_ustring()` call —
that is what the generator actually emits; the patch pins reality, not a prettier rewrite).
The old expectation is contradicted by the generator: the string is now a `UString` built from a
literal, not a `&str` literal with `.to_string()`.
It was failing before: before-48 census name `sealed variant sample trees carry the ruled
declaration and printed forms`; reproduced in my local before targeted run; the resume agent's
own targeted-1 (fail) → targeted-4 (pass) pair shows the flip.
Why the completed patch missed it: their §3.14 claims the file "contains no Rust-target
String/&str assertion" — true for a scan over the tokens `String`/`&str`, but line 245 asserts a
string-literal call site in the form `"literal".to_string()`, which a `String`-token scan misses.
The independent `string-expectation-scope` census (REPORT.md line 302) had counted this line in
its 47-line table (`sealed-variants 1`). **Inclusion is supported; this is a genuine fix the
completed 12-file patch does not contain.** The 3 other sealed-variants tests
(`@:sealed on a class...`, `self-construction with arguments...`, `the sanctioned
self-construction static...`) are fresh-generation spawn tests that time out environmentally in
every run (before and after, both worktrees) — not patch-relevant.

## 4. The other 3 files the brief omits from the resume patch (`std-string`, `string-buffer`,
`value-type`)
All three are in BOTH patches with identical hunks (all verified against gen:
std_string_ops.rs:35-36, string_buf_ops.rs:13, value_type_ops.rs:41-42). Not divergence points;
the brief's 10-file description is simply stale relative to the on-disk patch.

## 5. The one true behavioral divergence: `static-state.test.ts` final hunk (in both patches'
file set, conflicting edits)
- Completed: keeps `expect(content).not.toContain(" as ");` (line 53). Under this patch the test
  `static fields generated trees > Rust uses Mutex guards and the direct constant lane` FAILS
  (it is one of the completed agent's 35 remaining names).
- Resume: deletes that guard and substitutes
  `expect(content.replace(/as \*const \[u16\] as \*const [A-Za-z_:]+/g, "")).not.toContain(" as ")`
  with a 3-line comment calling the stripped lane "sanctioned". Under this patch the test PASSES
  (resume targeted-3 line 8; reproduced in my after targeted run, pending).
Supporting gen fact: `static_state_ops.rs:29` really contains
`pub const STATIC_STATE_OPS_EMPTY_MARK: &UStr = unsafe { &*(&[0x0065u16, 0x006Du16, 0x0070u16,
0x0074u16, 0x0079u16] as *const [u16] as *const crate::runtime::u_string::UStr) };` — so the old
guard is stale w.r.t. the current generator, and the resume regex strips exactly that lane (and
only that lane; the guard still covers every other ` as ` occurrence).
Assessment: the change is *supported by generator output* but it **weakens a test assertion**
(the hard rule "do not weaken any test" cuts against carrying it as-is). The completed agent
classified the same line as RESIDUAL and deliberately left the test red rather than loosen the
guard. This is the single line of the resume patch that should require an explicit sign-off
decision rather than be silently carried forward.
